description:
EVIDENCE: mission_identify_target defaults orbit_first=True (missions.py:1229, server.py:3830 and :4890, dispatch at missions.py:1775 / server.py:3663). Plans are priced through the SAME fuel integrator that flies them - dry_run (missions.py:1603-1672) calls fuel.preflight_gate(route, start, speed, ...) at :1636 and returns est_fuel_pct as gate['plan_fuel_pct'] (:1662) alongside the BINGO line (:1652). Measured cost of the default identify profile is 108-129% of the quad airframe's usable fuel, i.e. strictly greater than 100%, so the M4 preflight gate refuses the plan and NO identify pass is flyable on the quad with default parameters.
WHY THIS IS A DOCTRINE DECISION, NOT A BUG: every component is behaving correctly. The fuel model is right (it is the one proven to reach BINGO in ~23 min), the gate is right to refuse a plan it cannot complete, and the wide-to-narrow cross-cue orbit is real reconnaissance work that genuinely costs that much. What is wrong is the COMBINATION shipped as the default. Silently making the numbers fit would undo the honesty the fuel and gate work bought.
OPTIONS TO DECIDE BETWEEN (pick deliberately, record the reasoning):
 (a) flip the default to orbit_first=False, making the orbit an explicit opt-in the operator pays for knowingly;
 (b) keep orbit_first=True but shorten the default orbit (fewer revolutions / tighter radius / higher standoff) until the profile prices under a stated fraction of usable fuel;
 (c) assign identify to a longer-endurance airframe profile and let the quad refuse it honestly;
 (d) accept the refusal as correct doctrine and make the gate's rejection message say WHICH parameter to change and by how much.
REQUIRED: whichever is chosen, add a test that pins the default identify profile's priced fuel against the airframe's usable fuel so this cannot silently regress.
